WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.310 --> 00:00:03.440
SPEAKER: You can use the
Alerting feature in Google

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:07.760
Cloud Monitoring to be notified
when issues or failures occur

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:09.770
within your application.

00:00:09.770 --> 00:00:11.510
To create your first
alerting policy,

00:00:11.510 --> 00:00:15.470
look for the Create Alerting
Policy button on your dashboard

00:00:15.470 --> 00:00:19.800
or via the Alerting menu
at the top of the screen.

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:21.440
When you create
your first policy,

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:23.190
you'll begin by
giving it a name.

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:26.400
In this case, I'll
call it Test Policy.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:28.700
You may want to choose
a more descriptive name

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:33.590
given that this is included in
any notifications about alerts.

00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:36.230
Next, you select the
type of condition

00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:40.610
under which the policy will
trigger a notification.

00:00:40.610 --> 00:00:43.460
In this case, we'll use a
metric threshold condition,

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:46.010
which is to describe
a case where

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:48.440
I want to be alerted
when a metric crosses

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:52.700
a threshold for
a period of time.

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:53.810
I'm an App Engine user.

00:00:53.810 --> 00:00:57.200
And in this case, I want
to be alerted related

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:58.770
to an App Engine module.

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:02.630
So I'll select the App
Engine Module resource type.

00:01:02.630 --> 00:01:04.709
And I'm concerned
with a single module.

00:01:04.709 --> 00:01:07.280
So I'll say Single.

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:10.430
And then I'll choose the
module for the policy.

00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:14.310
In this case, it's
the Comments module.

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:15.840
I click Next.

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:19.800
Then I select the metric
that I want to evaluate.

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:22.860
In this case, I could've
chosen total responses

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:25.890
or 500 responses.

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:28.590
But I'm primarily
concerned right now

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:33.450
with response latencies,
95th percentile.

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:34.560
I select that metric.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:36.810
And I see a preview
of the metric

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:39.720
down the bottom of the screen,
which shows that metric's

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:42.740
performance over the last hour.

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:45.170
In this case, I
want to be alerted

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:48.470
if the 95th percentile
response latency

00:01:48.470 --> 00:01:52.790
is above 900 milliseconds.

00:01:52.790 --> 00:01:57.620
Again, I see a preview of
that threshold on the chart.

00:01:57.620 --> 00:01:59.870
And I'll choose to
be alerted if it

00:01:59.870 --> 00:02:04.580
stays above that level
for five minutes.

00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:05.180
And that's it.

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:07.290
I'll save my condition.

00:02:07.290 --> 00:02:10.250
Next, I should choose
how I want to be notified

00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:12.440
when the alert is triggered.

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:17.370
I can be notified via email,
SMS, or popular services,

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:22.010
such as PagerDuty, HipChat,
Campfire, or Slack.

00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:23.970
In this case,
we'll choose email.

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:25.550
And I simply enter
the email address.

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:31.530
And then save the alert.

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:35.510
Now I'll be notified if
my 95th percentile latency

00:02:35.510 --> 00:02:40.810
for my Comments module exceeds
the threshold for five minutes.

